WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.920 --> 00:00:04.760
I'm Jenna Fisher.

2
00:00:04.940 --> 00:00:06.060
And I'm Angela Kinsey.

3
00:00:06.200 --> 00:00:07.340
We were on The Office together.

4
00:00:07.580 --> 00:00:08.600
And we're best friends.

5
00:00:08.700 --> 00:00:12.420
And now we're doing the Ultimate Office rewatch podcast just for you.

6
00:00:12.580 --> 00:00:19.400
Each week, we will break down an episode of The Office and give exclusive behind-the-scenes stories that only two people who were there can tell you.

7
00:00:19.580 --> 00:00:20.560
We're The Office ladies.

8
00:00:21.020 --> 00:00:25.820
Hello, everyone.

9
00:00:26.360 --> 00:00:26.800
Hi.

10
00:00:27.460 --> 00:00:29.040
Guess what we're talking about today.

11
00:00:29.920 --> 00:00:32.020
I would do an impression, but it would be horrible.

12
00:00:32.360 --> 00:00:33.740
I was going to do like...

13
00:00:36.940 --> 00:00:43.680
That was me trying to be like one of the background singers in a cappella.

14
00:00:43.900 --> 00:00:45.580
Like the people that don't say words.

15
00:00:45.960 --> 00:00:47.200
The people that...

16
00:00:47.200 --> 00:00:47.300
Do the beat?

17
00:00:47.380 --> 00:00:48.900
Their sole job is like...

18
00:00:51.920 --> 00:00:53.140
How do you get that job?

19
00:00:53.420 --> 00:00:54.260
I don't know.

20
00:00:54.420 --> 00:00:56.520
I wouldn't get it because I can't carry a tune.

21
00:00:56.780 --> 00:00:59.920
Well, today, everybody, we're talking about Here Comes Treble.

22
00:00:59.920 --> 00:01:05.720
It is Season 9, Episode 5, written by Owen Ellickson, directed by Claire Scanlon.

23
00:01:06.260 --> 00:01:08.700
Here's what I learned when I watched this episode.

24
00:01:08.920 --> 00:01:09.100
Yeah.

25
00:01:09.440 --> 00:01:11.480
I found the a cappella singing delightful.

26
00:01:11.480 --> 00:01:13.240
I just loved every time they sang.

27
00:01:13.440 --> 00:01:15.000
I mean, I love a cappella.

28
00:01:15.120 --> 00:01:17.100
My husband was in an a cappella group.

29
00:01:17.200 --> 00:01:17.400
Yeah.

30
00:01:17.500 --> 00:01:19.480
We had to dig out some old videos.

31
00:01:19.740 --> 00:01:20.300
Oh, really?

32
00:01:20.400 --> 00:01:20.680
Oh, yeah.

33
00:01:20.820 --> 00:01:25.560
I watched a bunch of his group, In the Buff, from University of Colorado.

34
00:01:25.700 --> 00:01:25.980
Naked?

35
00:01:26.680 --> 00:01:27.120
What?

36
00:01:27.280 --> 00:01:28.340
No, that's their name.

37
00:01:28.480 --> 00:01:29.760
In the Buff is their name.

38
00:01:29.920 --> 00:01:31.980
They're the buffalos.

39
00:01:32.160 --> 00:01:33.360
It's like a play on words.

40
00:01:33.860 --> 00:01:36.980
You know, it really sounds like I'm going to watch Naked Singers.

41
00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:37.440
I know.

42
00:01:37.580 --> 00:01:38.040
I juSt...

43
00:01:38.040 --> 00:01:40.580
Did they think this through when they named their group?

44
00:01:40.840 --> 00:01:42.400
But I'm going to save this.

45
00:01:42.540 --> 00:01:47.720
I did look up a bunch of college a cappella group names, and a lot of them are a little

46
00:01:47.720 --> 00:01:48.240
racy.

47
00:01:48.620 --> 00:01:49.100
Really?

48
00:01:49.500 --> 00:01:49.550
Yeah.

49
00:01:50.120 --> 00:01:52.500
Well, why don't we start now with a summary?

50
00:01:52.900 --> 00:01:53.920
Here it is.

51
00:01:54.360 --> 00:01:59.320
Andy brings his college a cappella group to the office for a Halloween party performance.

52
00:02:00.040 --> 00:02:03.680
However, this is going to reignite his rivalry with Broccoli Rob.

53
00:02:04.700 --> 00:02:08.460
I mean, Broccoli Rob is kind of sneaky.

54
00:02:08.960 --> 00:02:09.320
Yeah.

55
00:02:09.540 --> 00:02:12.580
And I have some thoughts on his apartment, but we'll get to it.

56
00:02:12.660 --> 00:02:12.960
Oh, yeah.

57
00:02:12.960 --> 00:02:19.120
Meanwhile, in this episode, Pam and Jim struggle to agree on their level of financial participation

58
00:02:19.120 --> 00:02:22.840
in the sports marketing startup that Jim is now a part of.

59
00:02:23.040 --> 00:02:28.180
And Dwight starts an investigation to find the owner of an anti-anxiety pill he found on

60
00:02:28.180 --> 00:02:28.620
the floor.

61
00:02:28.620 --> 00:02:30.420
Fast fact number one.

62
00:02:30.620 --> 00:02:36.080
The storyline with Andy and his college a cappella group was inspired by our writer Halstead

63
00:02:36.080 --> 00:02:37.540
Sullivan's Real Life.

64
00:02:37.640 --> 00:02:43.360
Once again, Halstead's Real Life made it into an episode, but this one is fantastic.

65
00:02:43.720 --> 00:02:47.980
We reached out to Halstead and we asked him, how did this Andy storyline come about?

66
00:02:48.180 --> 00:02:50.400
He shares about his true life story.

67
00:02:50.580 --> 00:02:51.280
You got to hear it.

68
00:02:51.700 --> 00:02:52.760
Hey, Angela and Jenna.

69
00:02:53.200 --> 00:02:56.420
Thanks so much for asking me to tell your listeners about yet another office story that

70
00:02:56.420 --> 00:02:58.920
was loosely based on something that actually happened to me.

71
00:02:59.580 --> 00:03:02.300
Though, in this case, it's not so loose.

72
00:03:02.540 --> 00:03:06.200
To give you some brief history, when I was a student at the University of Virginia, I started

73
00:03:06.200 --> 00:03:10.360
an a cappella group called the Virginia Hullabahoos, and the group is still going strong today.

74
00:03:11.080 --> 00:03:13.600
Over the years, the Hullabahoos have sung all across the country.

75
00:03:13.820 --> 00:03:15.120
They've sung in Europe and the Philippines.

76
00:03:15.580 --> 00:03:18.740
They've sung at the White House for a couple of different presidents, and they were even in

77
00:03:18.740 --> 00:03:22.360
the first Pitch Perfect movie, where you can see them singing in the final countdown at

78
00:03:22.360 --> 00:03:22.980
the end of the movie.

79
00:03:23.700 --> 00:03:27.460
Now, needless to say, I'm extremely proud of all they've accomplished since I founded

80
00:03:27.460 --> 00:03:30.060
the group, oh, so very many years ago.

81
00:03:31.060 --> 00:03:34.520
And a couple of years before I was on the office, the current group of guys at the time came

82
00:03:34.520 --> 00:03:35.120
out to L.A.

83
00:03:35.380 --> 00:03:37.180
to sing the national anthem at a Lakers game.

84
00:03:37.640 --> 00:03:41.020
They asked if they could stay with me for a couple of days, and of course they said yes,

85
00:03:41.580 --> 00:03:44.140
even though it meant 14 college students staying in our house.

86
00:03:44.280 --> 00:03:44.980
And was it Hal?

87
00:03:45.660 --> 00:03:46.020
Absolutely.

88
00:03:46.960 --> 00:03:50.840
But since they were coming to L.A., I decided to set up a concert for them as well.

89
00:03:51.080 --> 00:03:54.260
I invited alumni and a ton of my friends, and it was a nice event.

90
00:03:54.400 --> 00:03:55.380
I even paid for the whole bar.

91
00:03:56.140 --> 00:03:59.840
To give you a little more backstory, since I was the founder of the group, historically,

92
00:04:00.080 --> 00:04:03.780
pretty much any time I showed up at a concert, the guys would invite me on stage to sing

93
00:04:03.780 --> 00:04:07.360
the song that I sang when I was in the group, which was a song called Run Around Sue.

94
00:04:08.800 --> 00:04:12.900
So before this concert, and I don't know what got into me, before the guys went on stage,

95
00:04:12.900 --> 00:04:17.140
I was like, guys, I know you're going to pull me up there to sing my signature song, but

96
00:04:17.140 --> 00:04:18.060
you really don't have to.

97
00:04:18.240 --> 00:04:18.980
This night is yours.

98
00:04:19.540 --> 00:04:20.520
Just go have fun.

99
00:04:21.200 --> 00:04:25.420
Fully knowing, in my heart of hearts, that of course they would invite me up to sing my

100
00:04:25.420 --> 00:04:25.720
song.

101
00:04:25.900 --> 00:04:27.980
I mean, it had never not happened before.

102
00:04:29.520 --> 00:04:34.440
So as I'm in the audience, enjoying the concert, and silently warming up my vocal cords, the

103
00:04:34.440 --> 00:04:37.940
guys are singing song after song after song, the audience is loving it, and I'm getting

104
00:04:37.940 --> 00:04:41.080
more and more nervous to be about to sing in front of all of my L.A.

105
00:04:41.220 --> 00:04:42.320
friends, and then

106
00:04:42.320 --> 00:04:47.400
suddenly, the concert's over, and they didn't invite me up to sing my song.

107
00:04:49.000 --> 00:04:50.300
I was livid.

108
00:04:50.740 --> 00:04:53.920
And was that a rational feeling for an adult man to have?

109
00:04:53.920 --> 00:04:54.440
No.

110
00:04:54.900 --> 00:04:59.540
But if you think any man who sings four-part harmony without instruments, and thinks that

111
00:04:59.540 --> 00:05:04.220
it's actually entertaining to people is rational, you are sorely mistaken, my friends.

112
00:05:04.700 --> 00:05:06.060
But the story doesn't end there.

113
00:05:06.220 --> 00:05:09.760
A few years after that, when we were trying to think of a story for Andy Bernard in Here

114
00:05:09.760 --> 00:05:13.720
Comes Trouble, I told this story in the writer's room, and it became an episode.

115
00:05:14.380 --> 00:05:19.160
And as we were writing, of course I suggested to Greg that the actual hullabahoos play the

116
00:05:19.160 --> 00:05:22.500
group Here Comes Trouble, and lo and behold, six of them got caSt.

117
00:05:22.500 --> 00:05:24.360
But the story doesn't end there.

118
00:05:24.820 --> 00:05:29.240
When they came out, I hosted all 14 guys in the group, and the guys who didn't get cast

119
00:05:29.240 --> 00:05:30.360
came out to set to watch.

120
00:05:30.620 --> 00:05:34.360
And on the last day of shooting, it was actually my birthday.

121
00:05:35.040 --> 00:05:40.420
And there on the set, the hullabahoos brought out a huge cake for me and sang happy birthday

122
00:05:40.420 --> 00:05:40.880
to me.

123
00:05:41.200 --> 00:05:46.940
And then, in front of the whole cast and crew of the office, they invited me up to sing

124
00:05:46.940 --> 00:05:48.140
my signature song.

125
00:05:49.000 --> 00:05:53.140
So, I guess if there's a moral to this story, it's that when life gives you lemons, and you

126
00:05:53.140 --> 00:05:57.480
don't get asked to sing your signature song, you make lemonade, and turn it into an office

127
00:05:57.480 --> 00:06:01.340
episode where you get to sing your song in front of your whole office family.

128
00:06:01.960 --> 00:06:06.020
But if you want to hear more, you can listen to the hullabahoos on Spotify and Apple Music.

129
00:06:06.280 --> 00:06:07.520
Sorry, I couldn't help it.

130
00:06:07.580 --> 00:06:08.720
I'm just so damn proud of them.

131
00:06:09.320 --> 00:06:09.700
Okay.

132
00:06:09.700 --> 00:06:10.660
I'm really done now.

133
00:06:10.900 --> 00:06:11.140
Thanks.

134
00:06:12.160 --> 00:06:15.060
Oh, and I love that story.

135
00:06:15.720 --> 00:06:17.980
I remember them singing on the stages.

136
00:06:18.300 --> 00:06:19.060
I do, too.

137
00:06:19.060 --> 00:06:24.800
And, you know, as I remember it, it was Claire's idea to have the happy birthday song to Halstead

138
00:06:24.800 --> 00:06:25.260
on set.

139
00:06:25.500 --> 00:06:26.400
Yes, it was.

140
00:06:26.560 --> 00:06:30.240
You know, we reached out to Claire for this episode, and one of the things she said was

141
00:06:30.240 --> 00:06:35.400
that this moment of them singing and Halstead singing was one of her favorite memories

142
00:06:35.400 --> 00:06:36.920
from this episode, and mine, too.

143
00:06:36.920 --> 00:06:41.980
I also love that she shared that she has a copy of it, and she gave it to Halstead, and

144
00:06:41.980 --> 00:06:43.680
he's the only one that has the copy.

145
00:06:43.820 --> 00:06:44.800
No one else has it.

146
00:06:45.100 --> 00:06:46.220
So, Halstead.

147
00:06:46.700 --> 00:06:47.120
Well.

148
00:06:47.320 --> 00:06:47.600
Well.

149
00:06:47.800 --> 00:06:52.540
We asked Halstead if we could share it, and he said no, because they sang it in a key too

150
00:06:52.540 --> 00:06:52.960
high.

151
00:06:54.000 --> 00:06:59.140
Well, Halstead, we would like to invite you, the office ladies, to sing Run Around Sue

152
00:06:59.140 --> 00:07:02.800
with the hullabahoos, and we will play it on Office Ladies.

153
00:07:03.060 --> 00:07:04.300
Oh, that would be a treat.

154
00:07:04.580 --> 00:07:06.400
We have a whole season left, Halstead.

155
00:07:06.920 --> 00:07:07.520
All right.

156
00:07:07.700 --> 00:07:12.620
Moving on to fast fact number two, you know the guy in the acapella group who ends up

157
00:07:12.620 --> 00:07:13.780
serenading Pam?

158
00:07:14.020 --> 00:07:14.780
Oh, yes.

159
00:07:15.000 --> 00:07:15.280
Mm-hmm.

160
00:07:15.480 --> 00:07:16.780
While Jim and Pam fight.

161
00:07:17.080 --> 00:07:17.340
Yes.

162
00:07:17.460 --> 00:07:19.060
It's such a fun scene.

163
00:07:19.300 --> 00:07:22.080
So, that singer is named Nick Caferro.

164
00:07:22.360 --> 00:07:22.700
Mm-hmm.

165
00:07:22.760 --> 00:07:26.340
He was not an actor at the time when they did this.

166
00:07:26.340 --> 00:07:28.260
He was just a member of the hullabahoos.

167
00:07:28.740 --> 00:07:34.140
Halstead suggested that we reach out to him to hear a little bit more about what it was

168
00:07:34.140 --> 00:07:40.100
like for all these guys to suddenly be invited to be on The Office, and Nick sent in an audio

169
00:07:40.100 --> 00:07:40.440
clip.

170
00:07:40.620 --> 00:07:41.260
We got to hear it.

171
00:07:41.260 --> 00:07:42.840
Office Ladies!

172
00:07:43.100 --> 00:07:43.700
Office Ladies!

173
00:07:43.700 --> 00:07:44.640
Oh, my goodness.

174
00:07:44.880 --> 00:07:47.740
I cannot believe I am recording this right now.

175
00:07:47.960 --> 00:07:50.620
This is such a thrill, such an honor.

176
00:07:50.620 --> 00:07:53.780
I'm so excited that you asked me to be part of this.

177
00:07:54.120 --> 00:08:00.820
I am a huge fan of the two of you, of this podcast, and of course, of The Office, and

178
00:08:00.820 --> 00:08:02.120
this is like a dream come true.

179
00:08:02.300 --> 00:08:04.260
Thank you so much for asking me to be part of this.

180
00:08:04.400 --> 00:08:06.200
So, I was a junior in college.

181
00:08:06.200 --> 00:08:07.640
I was at the University of Virginia.

182
00:08:08.420 --> 00:08:11.220
So, I remember I was the president of the group at the time.

183
00:08:11.220 --> 00:08:15.320
I got an email saying that they wanted us to audition for The Office.

184
00:08:15.320 --> 00:08:20.720
I thought it was a prank, but once I kind of got my heart rate down and figured out it

185
00:08:20.720 --> 00:08:22.060
was real, I got the group together.

186
00:08:22.240 --> 00:08:28.480
There were 18 members of us, and I put all of us on tape, and you know, looking back on

187
00:08:28.480 --> 00:08:31.400
it, I had zero idea what I was doing.

188
00:08:31.560 --> 00:08:35.060
So, these auditions are, they were all done in my dorm room.

189
00:08:35.460 --> 00:08:41.420
They have the worst lighting and background you could possibly imagine, and probably some

190
00:08:41.420 --> 00:08:43.160
of the worst acting you could possibly imagine.

191
00:08:43.160 --> 00:08:48.980
But we all did the scene one by one and sang a song because the role required us to sing.

192
00:08:49.240 --> 00:08:53.860
And we sang I'll Be by Edwin McCain, which is actually what I ended up singing on the

193
00:08:53.860 --> 00:08:54.640
show to you, Jenna.

194
00:08:55.800 --> 00:09:00.000
And I remember sending all the auditions into Halstead first before I sent them into the

195
00:09:00.000 --> 00:09:03.980
producers and casting, and Halstead was like, um, you need to redo these.

196
00:09:04.080 --> 00:09:04.560
These are terrible.

197
00:09:04.700 --> 00:09:09.560
So, we ended up redoing all of them, which was for the best, obviously.

198
00:09:09.940 --> 00:09:11.260
And then I sent them in.

199
00:09:11.260 --> 00:09:16.200
And a few days later, I got a call saying that we had been cast on an episode of The

200
00:09:16.200 --> 00:09:16.640
Office.

201
00:09:17.340 --> 00:09:20.980
And I was cast in the role that would be speaking and singing.

202
00:09:21.600 --> 00:09:23.660
And I almost threw up.

203
00:09:24.200 --> 00:09:28.420
Unfortunately, only seven of the 18 members in the Hullabahoos were asked to be on the

204
00:09:28.420 --> 00:09:28.660
show.

205
00:09:28.800 --> 00:09:33.560
But what we did is we took all the money that we were going to get paid, the seven of

206
00:09:33.560 --> 00:09:37.320
us, and we used that to buy plane tickets so that the rest of the group could come out

207
00:09:37.320 --> 00:09:39.400
and experience this, too, and have fun in L.A.

208
00:09:40.160 --> 00:09:42.440
Nick, that story is bonkers.

209
00:09:42.780 --> 00:09:44.900
I cannot imagine getting that email.

210
00:09:45.340 --> 00:09:47.160
But here's what I have to say.

211
00:09:47.860 --> 00:09:50.580
Why did we not fly these guys out?

212
00:09:50.920 --> 00:09:51.420
What?

213
00:09:51.820 --> 00:09:52.740
I don't know.

214
00:09:53.200 --> 00:09:57.580
I mean, I guess maybe we flew out the guys that got cast, and then they spent their money

215
00:09:57.580 --> 00:09:59.060
to fly out the other people.

216
00:09:59.120 --> 00:09:59.840
That's what it sounds like.

217
00:10:00.040 --> 00:10:03.760
Well, thank God, Angela, because when I first listened to that, I thought, what?

218
00:10:03.760 --> 00:10:04.680
No, no, no.

219
00:10:04.760 --> 00:10:07.840
I think they used their salaries to fly out the people.

220
00:10:07.840 --> 00:10:08.640
The rest of the group.

221
00:10:08.760 --> 00:10:09.860
Yes, which was very sweet.

222
00:10:09.900 --> 00:10:10.120
Classy move.

223
00:10:10.260 --> 00:10:10.800
Very classy.

224
00:10:11.040 --> 00:10:15.540
Well, in addition to meeting the entire Office cast while they were there, another thing

225
00:10:15.540 --> 00:10:21.680
that Nick shared with us was that Bryan Cranston was also walking around because he was there

226
00:10:21.680 --> 00:10:23.520
doing post-production on Workbus.

227
00:10:23.800 --> 00:10:24.940
And that really threw them.

228
00:10:24.940 --> 00:10:25.500
Yes.

229
00:10:25.840 --> 00:10:28.600
And they were like, wait, I thought we were coming to work on The Office.

230
00:10:28.700 --> 00:10:29.200
What is happening?

231
00:10:29.320 --> 00:10:30.320
Why is Walter White here?

232
00:10:30.440 --> 00:10:30.620
Yeah.

233
00:10:31.060 --> 00:10:33.840
Well, Nick shared a few more stories from his time on the set.

234
00:10:33.980 --> 00:10:35.460
We'll sprinkle those throughout this episode.

235
00:10:35.680 --> 00:10:38.520
And now I'm going to move us on to fast fact number three, Angela.

236
00:10:39.200 --> 00:10:41.460
Stephen Colbert as Broccoli Rob.

237
00:10:42.040 --> 00:10:45.620
I so believed Andy and Broccoli Rob's rivalry.

238
00:10:45.840 --> 00:10:46.900
I really believed it.

239
00:10:47.100 --> 00:10:49.500
Oh, Broccoli Rob is insidious.

240
00:10:49.900 --> 00:10:51.780
He's so gaslighty, isn't he?

241
00:10:52.260 --> 00:10:54.700
We had a fan question from Hannah Ellen, Arizona.

242
00:10:54.940 --> 00:10:59.880
Hannah, who said, did you guys get to meet Stephen Colbert or was he only on the video

243
00:10:59.880 --> 00:11:00.320
call?

244
00:11:00.700 --> 00:11:05.240
Well, first of all, Hannah, I'll say that Ed was our connection to Stephen Colbert from

245
00:11:05.240 --> 00:11:06.560
their time on The Daily Show.

246
00:11:07.140 --> 00:11:09.580
And unfortunately, we did not get to meet him.

